  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 302 AM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nAn upper level high will still reman over the area today with very \nhot temps as readings reach the upper 90's to lower 100's across \nnern CO.  Meanwhile, satellite imagery continues to show some influx \nof monsoon moisture across nrn portions of the CWA.  PW values are \nfcst to be around 150% above normal over the nrn mtns this aftn.  As \na result, will see sct showers and tstms across portions of the \nhigher terrain with localized areas of heavier rainfall as storms \nwill be slow moving.  Across nern CO, it will be dry this aftn. \nHowever, by early evening an outflow boundary may come off the \nhigher terrain so can't rule out a slight chc of showers and storms \nalong nrn portions of the I-25 Corridor.\n\nOn Tue, the upper level high will weaken a bit and shift some what\nto the southeast. As a result, this will allow for monsoon \nmoisture to move into most of the area with weak flow aloft. \nMeanwhile, a cool front will move across nern CO late tonight \ninto Tue morning with easterly low level flow. As a result, it \nwon't be as hot across the plains as readings drop into the upper \n80's to mid 90's for Tue aftn. As fas as precip, will see sct slow\nmoving showers and storms in the aftn over the higher terrain. PW\nvalues will be 150% to 175% above normal over the higher terrain \nso expect some heavier rainfall in some areas. As far as the \nplains, outflow boundaries from tstms over the higher terrain will\nmove across the I-25 Corridor by late aftn or early evening. As a\nresult, should see additional tstm activity develop by late aftn \nor early evening from Denver north to the WY border. With \nincreasing ESE low level flow Tue evening along with PW's above \n150% may see heavier rainfall across nrn portions of the I-25 \nCorridor.\n\nFor Wed, will continue to see monsoon moisture moving across the \narea as the main upper level high is centered to the southeast of \nthe area.  PW's across the high terrain will be from 150% to 200% \nabove normal so expect areas of heavier rainfall during the aftn \nwith the stronger storms.  Across the plains, the low level flow \nwill become southeast so expect a Denver cyclone to develop by aftn.\nShould see tstms develop along the associated convergence zone with \nstorms moving slowly to the east from late aftn thru the evening \nhours. Once again with PW's aoa 150% or normal expect areas of \nheavy rainfall. Highs on Wed, will remain in the upper 80's to mid\n90's over nern CO.\n\nBy Thu, monsoon moisture will continue over the area as a weak upper \nlevel trough moves across Thu aftn and night.  PW's are still fcst \nto be from 150% to 200% above normal.  Thus should see another round \nof showers and storms across much of the area with areas of heavy \nrainfall.  Highs on Thu will be in the upper 80's to lower 90's \nacross the plains.\n\nLooking ahead to Fri and Sat, the upper level high will gradually \nbuild back into the area as monsoon moisture decreases.  As a \nresult, will see lower tstm chances both days with warmer \ntemperatures. Highs across nern CO will rise back into the 95 to 100 \nrange by Sat.",
